  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "A dead tramp in a borrowed uniform fooled Hitler. Britain called it Operation Mincemeat, and it was exactly that cynical.",
        "visual_query": "wartime british naval officer uniform",
        "visual_fallback": "naval uniform"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The man was Glyndwr Michael, a homeless Welshman, dead of rat poison. No family was waiting on news of him.",
        "visual_query": "1940s london wet empty street",
        "visual_fallback": "wet street"
      },
      {
        "narration": "They built him a life: Captain William Martin, Royal Marines. A fianc\u00e9e, a ring receipt, an angry bank letter, theatre stubs.",
        "visual_query": "vintage letters documents desk lamp",
        "visual_fallback": "old letters"
      },
      {
        "narration": "On the thirtieth of April, 1943, a submarine released him into the sea off Huelva, Spain. His briefcase said the Allies were going for Greece.",
        "visual_query": "spanish atlantic coast fishing town",
        "visual_fallback": "atlantic coast"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Two weeks later, decrypted German traffic repeated the lie back. Panzers rolled toward Salonika. The landings hit Sicily.",
        "visual_query": "sicily coastline landing craft 1943",
        "visual_fallback": "sicilian coast"
      }
    ],
